## Machine-readable

```json
{
  "sir": {
    "displayName": "{{ owner_display_name or customer_name }}",
    "email": "{{ owner_email or customer_email or '' }}",
    "channels": {
      "slack":      { "userId": "", "dmChannelId": "" },
      "telegram":   { "chatId": "", "botAccount": "default" },
      "agentmail":  { "address": "{{ agentmail_inbox or '' }}" },
      "agentphone": { "e164": "{{ agentphone_number or '' }}" }
    }
  }
}
```

## How to use this file

When Sir tells you to deliver something to him on a channel ("send this to me on Slack",
"text me when it's done", "drop me an email"), use the cached IDs above. **Do not search
Slack's user directory, do not call Telegram `getUpdates`, do not page through email
contacts** — you'll waste turns and still not find what you need. The values here are
authoritative.

If a channel below isn't in the table (cell shows a placeholder like `_(populate after…)_`,
or the JSON value is empty), THEN fall back to discovery / pairing for that channel only.
Once you've found the value, ask Sir if you can save it here for next time — then
overwrite this file via `self({ endpoint: "/api/v1/admin/workspace/KNOWN_CONTACTS.md",
method: "PUT", body: { content: "<full markdown>" } })`.

See the `alfred-channel-delivery` skill for end-to-end `self()` call shapes per channel.
